WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.880
Let’s talk about Venezuela

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:05.080
and why it has two 
competing presidents.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:06.920
Juan Guaido is the 
one recognised by  

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:09.360
dozens of countries.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:14.240
But Nicolas Maduro is 
the one in charge.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:17.120
In parliament the opposition parties hold a majority. 

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.560
But elections are coming up

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.240
and the opposition is split over whether to boycott them.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.600
And while the 
politicians argue

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.120
people in Venezuela are struggling more than ever.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:38.360
So if things are so bad why does Maduro still hold power?

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:39.600
What could happen 
if the opposition  

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.880
decides to sit out 
the elections?

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.080
And why aren’t there big protests anymore?

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.040
For years we’ve been watching Venezuela climb to the top 

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:01.440
of just about every 
awful index there is.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.040
Worst economy 
in the world

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.240
the highest murder rate 

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.400
and a refugee crisis that
 rivals the one in Syria.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.480
That’s down to a combination of bad economic policy

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:18.320
corruption 

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.800
and in the last two 
years — sanctions.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.680
And this is how hard it is for people to feed themselves.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.880
Hyperinflation has made Venezuela’s currency

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.840
the bolivar, almost
 worthless.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:32.360
The minimum wage is 1.2 million bolivars a month.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:33.840
That’s just over a dollar.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.240
At that rate you’d have to work two months

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:38.040
to buy a carton of eggs.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.400
And prices go up 
every week.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:41.400
It’s a mess.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:46.000
But it wasn’t 
always this bad.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.080
Venezuela was once the place to be in Latin America.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:55.640
Its huge oil reserves helped build a booming economy.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.640
But over time corruption 
and inequality grew

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:03.200
and the shine started
 to wear off.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.440
Enter President 
Hugo Chavez. 

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.760
His social programmes 
were popular

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:11.920
but they relied on 
taking over businesses

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:13.240
controlling the currency 

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:16.560
and high oil prices which eventually crashed. 

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.520
So by the time 
Chavez died

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:20.480
his vice president, 
Nicolas Maduro

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.160
inherited an economy 
in a lot of trouble.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.160
Then 2018 happened. 

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.480
Maduro said he won 
the presidential race. 

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.560
But the opposition and a lot of countries disagreed. 

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:44.760
So the opposition basically said the presidency is vacant

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:46.320
and according to 
the constitution

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.400
the president of the parliament should take over 

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.320
and call new elections.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.160
That man was Juan Guaido.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:07.360
So for the last two years 

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:09.800
both men have laid claim
 to the presidency.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:11.040
Most opposition parties

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.760
consider Guaido to be the acting president.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.360
He’s also recognised by 
nearly 60 countries

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:19.760
including most of Latin America, the US and the EU.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:20.960
And then there’s Maduro

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:22.400
who has the backing 
of countries 

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:24.040
like Russia and China.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.360
And he’s the real authority 
on the ground.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:29.640
He has the loyalty
 of the military

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.680
the national guard and 
a civilian militia.

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:35.080
Military officials also
 control industries like

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:36.320
the state oil company 

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:38.960
and Maduro’s appointees 
run institutions like

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:40.520
the Supreme Court.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:41.520
And even though
 the government 

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.160
is nearly bankrupt 

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:45.400
Venezuela still manages
 to trade with

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.440
allies like Turkey and Iran.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:51.200
Guaido just can’t access those kinds of resources.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.040
But he does have
public support.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:55.240
Just not as much as before.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.200
In 2019 hundreds of 
thousands of people

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:00.280
rallied for months, 
backing Guaido.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:14.600
And after nearly 50 
 people were killed 

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:16.480
mostly by security forces

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.720
momentum fizzled out.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:24.080
Guaido’s coalition of opposition parties then tried

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:26.560
other ways to put 
pressure on Maduro. 

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.800
And they had help 
from abroad.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.560
Sanctions have blocked Venezuelan oil exports. 

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.600
Government assets worth billions of dollars  

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.280
have been frozen, including Venezuelan gold 

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:37.880
in the Bank of England.

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:39.440
Mercenaries have even tried 

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:40.240
and failed 

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:41.760
to overthrow Maduro.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:45.080
But after all that Maduro 
is still around.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:48.160
And his supporters say what the opposition has been doing

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:52.480
is evidence of an international conspiracy to remove Maduro.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:53.920
And here’s another thing.

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:55.720
Maduro is pretty unpopular. 

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:57.960
His disapproval rating according to one pollster  

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:00.040
is 77%.

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:02.160
But Guaido’s isn’t far behind.

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:16.360
And now there are parliamentary elections.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:18.120
Maduro says more 
than 100 parties 

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:19.520
have candidates running 

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:21.560
and he’s invited
 foreign observers.

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:23.960
But Guaido and some of the main opposition parties

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:26.640
say the elections have been rigged from the start.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:29.800
Opposition leaders have been jailed or simply removed 

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:32.600
and several political parties have been banned.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:35.360
They also say the body that runs elections is made up of 

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:38.600
people appointed by the pro-Maduro Supreme Court.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:41.560
So instead Guaido and the main opposition parties

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:42.880
are doing their own thing.

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:44.560
They’re calling on
people to boycott  

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:46.160
the election in December.

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:48.960
And in the meantime they’re holding their own referendum.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:51.480
It’s to ask people if they approve of what Maduro’s

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:53.280
doing and if they 
want him gone.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:03.800
But some just 
aren't convinced.

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:07.360
They say it’s better to run and try to win a seat in parliament.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:09.120
Either way Maduro 
stands to win  

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:11.040
control of basically everything.

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:12.520
And people who 
want change

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:15.680
haven’t been protesting 
the way they used to.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:17.840
They’re in the middle 
of a pandemic. 

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:20.960
There’s a state of emergency.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:24.200
There’s also a lot of fear.

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:36.000
So the protests may be smaller but the anger is growing.

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:06.680
A lot of people also seem
  to have just given up.

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:09.440
Many have lost faith in politicians, protests  

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:10.960
and elections.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:13.320
Whether Juan Guaido can convince people to boycott

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:15.760
the vote and take part 
in his referendum 

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:18.120
may give us an idea of just how much hope  

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:22.320
is actually left.

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:25.360
And while you’re here make sure to subscribe, like

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:27.960
or follow Al Jazeera wherever you’re watching this video

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:29.760
to stay on top of 
world news.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:30.840
And I’ll see you 
next week. 

